{"parentUuid":"3d93a30f-4ab0-450b-92d2-ad0ae8e45ca6","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a56ba8ba-7fe7-47ae-87db-446727729236","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LhiuaemiVrssvmXX9cPhWs","type":"tool_result","content":"commit 8f6de373c61ad6d475027f81f9ff22cd82de7736\nAuthor: Brandon Fryslie <demo@example.com>\nDate:   Mon Oct 5 23:15:55 2026 -0600\n\n    turnstart: a turn the voice opens cuts nothing off until Whisper hears words in it (hands-barge-in-wmz) (#229)\n    \n    * turnstart: a turn the voice opens cuts nothing off until Whisper hears words in it (hands-barge-in-wmz)\n    \n    The edge that opened a hold travels with the gate onto every frame and onto the\n    hold's TurnOpened and TurnResolved. EdgeTurnStart, the pipeline's one start\n    strategy, starts the turn as the hold opens for the held key, the phone's\n    button, and the wake word, and only on Whisper's words for engaged\n    conversation (turn_start in trigger.py). A voice-opened hold with no words\n    starts no turn, interrupts nothing, and is a FalseBargeIn line on the audit.\n    \n    * turnstart: every hold starts its turn; a voice-opened one cuts hands off on its words (hands-barge-in-wmz)\n    \n    Starting a voice-opened turn on its words moved Pipecat's interruption into the middle of the\n    turn's own data. It dropped the screen note pushed behind the words and could drop a later\n    hold's words. Floor, the quiet cue player and the latency window also lost the user who was\n… 17 more lines","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"587b0740-f3bd-482f-b25d-d23868e83765","timestamp":"2026-10-08T07:13:42.518Z"}
{"parentUuid":"a55a6560-e96f-4f1c-acd8-f8bf8b3a8953","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_011CfpMcn5kW4nJcumqmsBqJ","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-5-5","stop_reason":"tool_use","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":""}],"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2107}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"234091c4-29af-494b-968b-67142a5eee9d","timestamp":"2026-10-08T07:13:44.276Z"}
{"parentUuid":"234091c4-29af-494b-968b-67142a5eee9d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_011CfpMcn5kW4nJcumqmsBqJ","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-5-5","stop_reason":"tool_use","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BCUAXrDx361mm22AfhvzUm","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/groom/o6o.txt","content":"Cutting in while hands is speaking stops it less often than it used to: the user speaks over hands and hands keeps talking, or stops late. Barge-in is how the user takes the floor back, so every miss makes hands talk over them.\n\nLead, unverified: the report came a day after hands-barge-in-wmz (#229, commit 8f6de37), which made a turn opened by the voice (engaged conversation) cut nothing until Whisper hears words in it; a voice-opened hold that heard no words is a FalseBargeIn line on the audit. A short interjection Whisper drops, or transcribes late, would read to the user as a missed or late barge-in. Find the misses in the audit log first (src/hands/sessions/audit.py names what each hold left) and confirm or rule this out before changing anything; the trigger in use at each miss (talk key, wake word, engaged) is part of the answer.\n\nKeep #229's guarantee: a sound that is not the user speaking — hands' own echo, a cough — still cuts nothing.\n\nDone when:\n- The cause of the misses found in the audit log is named on this ticket, with the audit lines that show it.\n- A test in the in-process turn rig (tests/test_voice_turns.py) reproduces that miss — the user's words over hands' speech, shaped as in the log — and hands stops speaking in it; the wordless voice-opened hold test from #229 still cuts nothing.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2107}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"23333283-2939-44fc-8c87-44c65c6a1cba","timestamp":"2026-10-08T07:13:49.276Z"}
