{"parentUuid":"dbe66216-f302-4914-bc90-7b202316dcb5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"3fa17114-5577-44ce-9427-9e226b9746bf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tt8cHbhMT3PcdTVUofpdt2","type":"tool_result","content":"Creating tickets (lit)\n\nCreate tickets: \u0060lit new --title \"...\" --topic <topic> --type task|bug|feature|chore|epic [--parent <parent-id>] [--top]\u0060\n\n<agent-instructions>\u0060--description\u0060: describe what to build and why; leave how to the implementer — write what survives a refactor of the code it concerns.</agent-instructions>\n<agent-instructions>\u0060--topic\u0060: Required immutable issue topic slug (1-2 words; stable area of focus; e.g., 'refactor' or 'field-history')</agent-instructions>\n<agent-instructions>New tickets append to the bottom of their frame by default — the bottom of their epic's children, or of the top-level order for a ticket with no parent. Filing work records it; promoting it is a separate, deliberate act. Pass \u0060--top\u0060 at creation for a ticket that genuinely belongs ahead of its siblings — first among its epic's children, or first in the top-level order for a ticket with no parent; \u0060lit rank <id> --top\u0060 does the same later. A batch authored in order keeps that order with no flag.</agent-instructions>\n\nCreate a follow-up parented to a just-closed ticket: \u0060lit followup --on <closed-id> --title \"...\"\u0060 — a good habit for capturing work you surface as a child ticket while the context is fresh.\n\nCreating (or updating) several tickets at once: \u0060lit import --path <file.yaml>\u0060 reads a\nmulti-document YAML file, one ticket per document — a document without an \u0060id\u0060 creates,\none with an \u0060id\u0060 updates that existing ticket. Cheaper than N separate \u0060lit new\u0060/\u0060lit\nupdate\u0060 invocations f\n… 1 more lines","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5b72ec5c-c230-45e7-8df4-30c5277d865c","timestamp":"2026-10-09T22:35:23.127Z"}
