{"parentUuid":"3e0ebb5e-b9fc-42c6-8b42-5604d9af3fb0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_011CfoJ6gVxtc9N6EqDNbcRo","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-5-5","stop_reason":"tool_use","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017Tg8DkGRPgSFiEir6ar5XM","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"lit show hands-monetization-76j.3aj | sed -n '/description:/,/Epic:/p'; lit show hands-install-dye.kee | sed -n '/description:/,/Epic:/p'","description":"Read the signed-app and install tickets"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":230}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"2309516f-e65a-4cb7-9223-d62b5d6eda95","timestamp":"2026-10-07T17:47:00.655Z"}
{"parentUuid":"2309516f-e65a-4cb7-9223-d62b5d6eda95","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"00daf22d-a579-4117-9557-0b5b5ba8418a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017Tg8DkGRPgSFiEir6ar5XM","type":"tool_result","content":"description:\nThe one thing a paid hands can sell is not having to do the README's nine install steps or the manual updates (design-docs/monetization/hosted-subscription.md). Ship hands as a signed, notarized macOS app that puts its pieces in place, keeps them current, and checks a subscription. Users still bring their own Claude plan or API key, and hands never holds Claude credentials or bundles Claude usage; the terms ticket under this epic is what makes that true. Done when, on a fresh Mac (the hands-fresh tart VM), installing the app and signing in leads to a spoken turn with no terminal steps other than Claude Code's own install and login.\n\nEpic: hands-monetization-76j — Monetization\ndescription:\nREADME.md steps 1-3 have a person on a new Mac run Claude Code's installer, install Homebrew when missing and put brew on PATH, install PortAudio and uv, look up the newest release number, run \u0060uv tool install\u0060 with that release's constraints, run \u0060uv tool update-shell\u0060, and open a new terminal. All of it is typing hands can do. This ticket adds the epic's one command: a single line from README.md that a person pastes into Terminal on a Mac with none of these things, and that ends with Claude Code, PortAudio, uv and the newest released hands installed. Every later child of this epic extends this same command.\n\nThe only input allowed is one the OS requires (an administrator password, if the path taken needs one), announced with what it is for before it is asked. The person never \n… 10 more lines","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e7e2f081-080c-4bf7-83b9-334f06d3432e","timestamp":"2026-10-07T17:47:01.873Z"}
{"parentUuid":"efed4bd1-fdc2-41f5-ad0f-53da3844adf5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_011CfoJ6xcL2AUzCt99kn4J9","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-5-5","stop_reason":"end_turn","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":""}],"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1492}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"7abea371-9daf-449c-ba80-ad6f2939e0c8","timestamp":"2026-10-07T17:47:16.290Z"}
{"parentUuid":"7abea371-9daf-449c-ba80-ad6f2939e0c8","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_011CfoJ6xcL2AUzCt99kn4J9","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-5-5","stop_reason":"end_turn","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No, not in one step. Notarization only removes the security warning. It doesn't remove the permissions macOS makes the person grant by hand.\n\n**What \"notarized\" means:** hands gets signed with an Apple Developer ID (the Developer Program is \u002499/yr) and uploaded to Apple, which scans it and approves it. When someone downloads it and double-clicks, macOS opens it normally. Without that, they get \"can't be opened because Apple cannot check it for malware\" and have to go into System Settings to override it.\n\n**What a person would still do, even with a perfect notarized app:**\n1. **Download it** and drag it to Applications (or run a \u0060.pkg\u0060 installer).\n2. **Open it once.** macOS asks \"downloaded from the internet, open?\", one click.\n3. **Allow the microphone**, one click on a system prompt.\n4. **Turn on Input Monitoring for the talk key.** This is the worst step: macOS shows no allow button for it. The person goes to System Settings → Privacy & Security → Input Monitoring and flips the switch themselves, and the app usually has to restart afterwards. No app can grant this to itself.\n5. **Install and log in to Claude Code.** The ticket deliberately leaves this to the person, because hands must never hold their Claude credentials.\n\nSo it's about five acts: two clicks, a trip to System Settings, and Claude Code's own setup.\n\n**The useful comparison is the install epic, not zero steps.** \u0060hands-install-dye\u0060 already aims at one pasted Terminal command, ending with Input Monitoring as th\n… 8 more lines"}],"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1492}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"b226a422-8705-41db-8289-2761e6aecfa5","timestamp":"2026-10-07T17:47:18.486Z"}
